// The arena package: a dynamic region. An Arena value owns blocks of memory;
// values are allocated into it one after another and never freed one by one;
// when the Arena dies, every value in it that needs finalizing is finalized,
// the newest first, and then all its blocks are freed together.
//
//   mod scene;
//   import arena use *;
//
//   fn build() {
//     mut a = Arena();
//     imm root = a.alloc(Node(0, null));
//     imm leaf = a.alloc(Node(1, root as *Node));
//     ...
//   }   // 'a' dies here: leaf's death, then root's, then the blocks
//
// Allocation is a method call on the arena, 'a.alloc(value)', which moves the
// value into the arena and returns a '&mut' to it there. A reference into an
// arena costs nothing to copy and nothing to drop: no count, no move. The same
// is written 'new a[Node](0, null)', the arena value in the head and the type in
// brackets (a 'mut' before it may be written), which the compiler reads as that
// call: tests/newin.cone.
//
// A SCRATCH ARENA is an Arena held in a local variable. Its values live as
// long as that local does, until the function returns (or the block holding
// it ends), and each reference to one is a borrow with that local's lifetime,
// so it may be passed down to any function called while the scratch lives, or
// stored in another value of the same scratch, and needs no count, no key and
// no lock. A function called with the scratch ('&mut Arena') can allocate into
// it for its caller. The compiler checks these references as it checks any
// borrow: returning one, or a value holding one, from the function holding
// the scratch, or keeping one past the scratch in a variable and using it, is
// refused (test/cases/region/region_flow_arena_escape.cone pins each route).
// tests/scratch.cone runs what a scratch does, and examples/scratch.cone is
// one at work:
//
//   fn mostFrequent(text &Array[u8]) Answer {
//     mut s = Arena();                 // the scratch
//     imm seen = s.alloc(List[&mut Tally]());
//     ...count(&mut s, seen, word)...        // lent to the functions that work
//     Answer(best.word.clone(), seen.len());  // only values of its own leave
//   }   // every value in 's' dies here, then its blocks are freed
//
// An allocation loans nothing of the arena: Arena declares 'NoLoanMut' (a
// provisional name), its promise that it never moves what it lent. So any
// number of allocations may be live at once, and the arena may be allocated
// from, read and borrowed while they are; a value allocated in it may even
// hold a '&mut' of it. What an allocation held in a local keeps is the
// arena's life: while one is still used, 'consume(a)', '{ imm b = a; }',
// 'a <-> other' and 'a = Arena()' are refused
// (test/cases/region/region_flow_arena_freeze.cone).
//
// A borrow into a scratch held inside another value is checked as the bare
// borrow is: returning 'Holder(a.alloc(v))', an 'Option' holding one, or
// one read back out of a list ('*seen[0usize]') is refused, and so is keeping
// such a value past the scratch and using it.
//
// A DYNAMIC ARENA, 'DynArena', is one whose values outlive the function that
// made it: it may be returned, stored and passed, and its values go with it.
// A reference into it cannot be a borrow, which ends with a scope, so it is a
// KEY: a reference of the arena's invariant lifetime, ''=a', which reaches
// nothing on its own. The arena is the lock: 'ar[key]' reaches what the key
// names, and only where the arena and the key carry the same invariant
// lifetime, which the compiler checks as an identity, at no cost at run time
// ('ar[key]' is the key's pointer). Each call of 'DynArena.empty()' mints a
// fresh one, so a key of one arena never unlocks another:
//
//   struct Node['=a] { pub val i32; pub next Option[&'=a mut Node['=a]]; }
//   struct Chain['=a] { pub ar DynArena['=a]; pub first Option[&'=a mut Node['=a]]; }
//
//   fn build(n i32) Chain['=a] {
//     mut ar = DynArena.empty();          // a brand of its own
//     mut head Option[&'=a mut Node['=a]] = None[&'=a mut Node['=a]];
//     ...head = Some(ar.alloc(Node(i, head)));...   // keys, not borrows
//     Chain(ar, head);                // the arena leaves with its keys
//   }
//
//   fn sum(c &Chain['=a]) i32 { ... c.ar[key].val ... c.ar[key].next ... }
//
// 'ar[key]' reads (a '&T' of the value, '[]'), and in set position --
// 'ar[key] = v', 'ar[key].f = v', 'ar[key].f += 1', a method taking 'self: &mut'
// -- writes, through '&[]', the value's '&mut'. A key is copied freely, and a
// value it names may hold keys, of its own arena or another. What a key names
// lives as long as its arena, past every scope, so it holds no borrow: a key
// to a value that does is refused (ErrorKeyBorrow). examples/chain.cone
// builds and walks a linked list this way, and tests/dynamic.cone runs every
// rule; the compiler's suite pins each refusal
// (test/cases/lifetime/lifetime_inv_*.cone): another arena's key, a key used
// without its arena, a key kept past the loop pass whose arena made it.
//
// Memory. An arena grows by blocks whose room doubles: the first has 4096 bytes
// of room for values, each block after it twice the room of the one before, up
// to 1 MiB, and every block after that 1 MiB (so a block count grows about
// logarithmically to 1 MiB and linearly after, and the arena holds at most about
// twice what it uses, plus a 1 MiB block's slack). A value needing more than the
// next block's room gets a block of its own size, and the doubling goes on where
// it was. 'withRoom(bytes)' makes the first block 'bytes' (4096 at least) and
// doubles from that, to 1 MiB. A block comes from the raw blocks layer (core's 'RawBlocks'). If that fails,
// or the room asked for cannot be counted in a usize, the arena panics. There
// is no fallible form of 'alloc' yet ('trynew a[T](...)' is refused).
//
// An arena is a REGION: the first field of an Arena, 'region', is a
// '*MemRegion', the arena's own allocate, resize and release, which a growable
// collection made in it calls instead of the C library. So 'new a[List[i32]](16)'
// puts the list's four words in the arena and its block there too, and so does
// a struct declaring CarriesRegion that is made in it ('new a[Mesh](50)': its
// lists draw from the arena when its construction names no other region). The
// region is an object of its own that the Arena points to, so it stays where it
// is when the Arena is moved, returned or stored with the lists in it.
//   - allocate bumps, as 'alloc' does;
//   - resize grows a block in place when it is the last thing the arena
//     allocated and the room is there. Otherwise it copies and abandons the old
//     one, which the arena does nothing more with until it dies: arenas leak by
//     design, and the larger the block a list outgrows, the more is held (two
//     lists pushed in turn copy at every growth). When the arena's current block
//     has no room for the new place, the block taken for it is twice the size
//     asked, so a lone list that keeps doubling finds the room for its next
//     doubling where it is, and is copied once for every block it fills, not at
//     every doubling (100,000 values of 8 bytes: five copies, six blocks);
//   - release does nothing.
// 'new a[T](...)' builds the value where it stays in the arena: the arguments of
// the construction are evaluated, the room is taken, and the construction runs
// there (see 'alloc'). So what the construction itself allocates in the arena, a
// list's block, comes after the value in memory, and a list made with a starting
// size has its block last, where it grows in place.
//
// A collection in an arena is finalized by the arena's list of finalizers, like
// any value: its 'final' gives its own elements' memory back to wherever it
// came from (a 'List[String]' whose strings came from the heap frees them when
// the arena dies), and its block goes with the arena's blocks.
//
// How a value is finalized. A value whose type needs finalizing
// ('mem.needsFinal[T]()') gets a small header just before it, allocated in
// the same bump: the next header in the arena's list, and a pointer to the
// function that finalizes a T at a given address. The arena keeps the list
// newest first, so teardown walks it in reverse order of allocation, as a
// function's locals die in reverse order of declaration. (The header goes on the
// list when the value has been built, so a value is newer on the list than
// anything its construction or its arguments allocated in the arena, though it
// lies before them in memory.) That function is the
// finalizer in T's type record ('mem.typeRecord[T]().finalize'), which runs
// what 'mem.finalize[T]' runs: the
// same death the compiler runs for a T anywhere, the value's own 'final', then
// each field's finalizer, recursively, then the owning references its fields
// hold are released (a 'Uni' one finalized and freed, an 'Rc' one counted
// down and finalized only if it was the last owner); a value typed as an enum
// dies as the variant it holds. A value whose type needs
// no finalizing costs no header and no list entry: nothing is run for it.
// A reference to another arena value is a borrow, and finalizing does nothing
// through a borrow, so every arena value is finalized once, by its own header.
//
// During teardown every block stays allocated until the last value has been
// finalized, so a 'final' that reads another value of the same arena reads
// live memory. The newer one may already be finalized; the older one is not
// yet. A 'final' that allocates into the arena it is dying in (through a
// reference it holds to it) is allowed: its value is finalized too, before
// the blocks go, since each header is taken off the list before its thunk
// runs.
//
// A value assigned over through the reference ('*r = v') is finalized as it
// is replaced, as any value stored over is; the arena finalizes whatever the
// slot holds when it dies. Assigning over the arena itself ('a =
// Arena()') tears the old one down first.

mod arena;

import libc;

// The header before each value that needs finalizing
struct FinalNode {
  pub next: *FinalNode;
  pub run: &fn(p: *u8);
}

// The header before each block: the block allocated before it, which teardown
// follows to free them all
struct Block {
  pub prev: *Block;
}

// One value the arena holds that a collector must trace (its type holds a traced
// reference, 'mem.holdsTraced[T]()'): the list of them is what it gives a collector. The
// node is allocated in the arena beside the value, whose address and type record
// it keeps, so the arena, which holds values of many types, gives a collector
// each of them as an Erased value, which is traced by its type's own trace.
struct GcTraceNode {
  pub next: *GcTraceNode;
  pub p: *u8;              // an Erased value is these two fields, 'p' and 'rec', as they sit here
  pub rec: *TypeRecord;
}

// A cursor over the values an arena holds that need tracing, newest first: 'next'
// gives a writable reference to each, as an Erased value
pub struct ArenaGcTraceItems is Iterator[&mut Erased] {
  node: *GcTraceNode;

  pub fn next(self: &mut) Option[&mut Erased] {
    if node as usize == 0usize { return None; }
    let at = node;
    node = (*at).next;
    Some[&mut Erased]((at as usize + mem.sizeof[usize]()) as &mut Erased)
  }
}

// Room the first block has for values, unless a value needs more, and the least a
// block made by 'withRoom' has
const blockBytes = 4096usize;

// The most room a block gets by doubling: each block after the first has twice the
// room of the one before it, until it has this much, and every block after that
// has this much too (unless a value needs more)
const maxBlockBytes = 1048576usize;

// An arena's state: its region and the blocks it bumps through. It is its own
// heap object, which the Arena points to and every collection made in the arena
// points to as its region ('header' is first, so the object is the region), so
// that moving the Arena moves neither.
struct ArenaCore is GcTraceElements {
  header: MemRegion;    // the region: allocate, resize and release, below
  blocks: *Block;       // the newest block, or null
  top: usize;           // the address of its first unused byte; 1 (with 'limit' 0) before the first block, so no value fits and nothing is placed at null
  limit: usize;         // the address just past its end
  nextRoom: usize;      // the room the next block gets unless a value needs more; doubles with each block that gets it, to maxBlockBytes
  finals: *FinalNode;   // the newest header, or null
  nfinals: usize;       // how many headers the list has held
  nblocks: usize;       // how many blocks it holds
  traced: *GcTraceNode;   // the newest value needing a trace, or null
  ntraced: usize;       // how many it has held

  // Room for a value of 'size' bytes aligned to 'align' that needs no
  // finalizing: its address. The caller writes the value there.
  fn place(self: &mut, size: usize, align: usize) *u8 inline {
    self.bump(0usize, align, size) as *u8
  }

  // Room for a value of 'size' bytes aligned to 'align' whose death the
  // arena will run (linkFinal), with room for its header just before it: its
  // address. The header is not on the list yet, so the value is built in
  // place first. Whatever that construction allocates in the arena lies after
  // the value in memory and goes onto the list before it, so it is finalized
  // after the value, as a value it was built from is.
  fn reserveFinal(self: &mut, size: usize, align: usize) *u8 inline {
    let hdr = mem.sizeof[FinalNode]();
    var al = align;
    if al < mem.alignof[FinalNode]() { al = mem.alignof[FinalNode](); }
    self.bump(hdr, al, size) as *u8
  }

  // The value at 'at', which reserveFinal made room for and which is built
  // now, is put on the list of values the arena finalizes, first: its death is
  // 'run', called with its address when the arena dies.
  fn linkFinal(self: &mut, at: *u8, run: &fn(p: *u8)) {
    let node = (at as usize - mem.sizeof[FinalNode]()) as *FinalNode;
    mem.writeRaw(node, FinalNode(finals, run));
    finals = node;
    nfinals = nfinals + 1usize;
  }

  // The values it holds that need tracing, for a collector (see Arena.gcTraceElements)
  pub fn gcTraceElements(self: &mut) ArenaGcTraceItems { ArenaGcTraceItems(traced) }

  // A new arena state: no block yet, the three region functions set
  fn make() *MemRegion {
    let core = libc.malloc(mem.sizeof[ArenaCore]()) as *ArenaCore;
    if core as usize == 0usize { panic("arena: out of memory for an arena"); }
    mem.writeRaw(core, ArenaCore(MemRegion(&regionAlloc, &regionResize, &regionRelease, &regionSize),
      null, 1usize, 0usize, blockBytes, null, 0usize, 0usize, null, 0usize));
    core as *MemRegion
  }

  // The value at 'p', whose type's record is 'rec', needs tracing: its node is
  // allocated here, and put first on the list
  fn noteTraced(self: &mut, p: *u8, rec: *TypeRecord) {
    let node = self.place(mem.sizeof[GcTraceNode](), mem.alignof[GcTraceNode]()) as *GcTraceNode;
    mem.writeRaw(node, GcTraceNode(traced, p, rec));
    traced = node;
    ntraced = ntraced + 1usize;
  }

  // Room for a value of 'size' bytes aligned to 'align', with 'hdr' bytes
  // just before it for its header, if it has one: the value's address. The
  // header ends where the value starts, whatever the value's alignment, since
  // 'align' is then at least the header's and the header's size is a multiple
  // of its alignment, so the header is aligned too.
  //
  // This is the fast path, inline in each allocation: round up, compare with
  // 'limit', store 'top'. With no block yet 'top' is 1 and 'limit' 0, so nothing
  // fits, a zero-sized value included, and the first allocation goes to bumpSlow.
  fn bump(self: &mut, hdr: usize, align: usize, size: usize) usize inline {
    let at = roundUp(top + hdr, align);
    if at + size <= limit {
      top = at + size;
      at
    }
    else {
      self.bumpSlow(hdr, align, size)
    }
  }

  // The same when the current block has no room (or there is none): a new
  // block, and the value at its start
  fn bumpSlow(self: &mut, hdr: usize, align: usize, size: usize) usize {
    self.grow(hdr + size + align);
    let at = roundUp(top + hdr, align);
    top = at + size;
    at
  }

  // A new block with room for at least 'need' bytes, made the one allocation
  // bumps through. What was left of the one before is not used again. Its room is
  // 'nextRoom', which doubles for the one after (to maxBlockBytes) when it was
  // enough; a value needing more gets a block of that size and leaves 'nextRoom'
  // as it was, so the doubling resumes where it was.
  fn grow(self: &mut, need: usize) {
    var room = nextRoom;
    if need > room { room = need; }
    else if room < maxBlockBytes {
      nextRoom = room * 2usize;
      if nextRoom > maxBlockBytes { nextRoom = maxBlockBytes; }
    }
    let blk = newBlock(blocks, room);
    blocks = blk;
    nblocks = nblocks + 1usize;
    top = blk as usize + mem.sizeof[Block]();
    limit = top + room;
  }

  // The block 'block', 'old' bytes long, made 'new' bytes (the region's
  // resize). The last thing the arena allocated grows, or shrinks, where it
  // is, if the room is there. Any other is allocated again, the bytes copied,
  // and the old one abandoned: the arena does nothing more with it until it dies.
  // When the new place does not fit the current block, the block taken for it is
  // twice the request, so a block that keeps growing finds the room for its next
  // doubling where it is, and is copied once for every block it fills, not at
  // every growth.
  fn resize(self: &mut, block: *u8, oldBytes: usize, newBytes: usize, align: usize) *u8 {
    if block as usize == 0usize { return self.place(newBytes, align); }
    let at = block as usize;
    if at + oldBytes == top && at + newBytes <= limit {
      top = at + newBytes;
      return block;
    }
    if roundUp(top, align) + newBytes > limit {
      self.grow(doubled(newBytes + align));
    }
    let moved = self.place(newBytes, align);
    var n = oldBytes;
    if newBytes < oldBytes { n = newBytes; }
    libc.memcpy(moved, block, n);
    moved
  }

  // Its death: every value that needs it finalized, newest first, each header
  // taken off the list before its value's thunk runs, so a value allocated by
  // a 'final' during teardown is finalized too, and none twice; then every
  // block freed. The blocks all stay until the last finalizer has run.
  fn teardown(self: &mut) {
    while finals as usize != 0usize {
      let node = finals;
      let hdr = node as &FinalNode;
      finals = hdr.next;
      let run = hdr.run;
      run((node as usize + mem.sizeof[FinalNode]()) as *u8);
    }
    while blocks as usize != 0usize {
      let blk = blocks;
      blocks = (blk as &Block).prev;
      RawBlocks.release(blk as *u8);
    }
  }
}

// The arena's region, as MemRegion's functions: each is handed the region,
// which is the ArenaCore it begins
fn coreOf(r: *MemRegion) &mut ArenaCore {
  (r as *ArenaCore) as &mut ArenaCore
}

fn regionAlloc(r: *MemRegion, bytes: usize, align: usize) *u8 {
  coreOf(r).place(bytes, align)
}

fn regionResize(r: *MemRegion, block: *u8, oldBytes: usize, newBytes: usize, align: usize) *u8 {
  coreOf(r).resize(block, oldBytes, newBytes, align)
}

// Nothing is done with a block given back: the arena's blocks go together
fn regionRelease(r: *MemRegion, block: *u8, bytes: usize) {}

pub struct Arena is Default, NoLoanMut, GcTraceElements {
  // The arena's region, which the collections made in it point to: public so
  // that 'new a[T](...)' can hand it to what it makes (typelit.c). Not to be
  // changed.
  pub region: *MemRegion;

  // An arena holding nothing, with no block until the first value arrives
  pub fn init(self: &new) {
    *self = Arena(ArenaCore.make());
  }

  // An arena holding nothing yet, whose first block is allocated now, with
  // room for at least 'bytes' of values and their headers: a scratch whose
  // need is known then makes one allocation rather than several
  pub fn withRoom(bytes: usize) Arena {
    let region = ArenaCore.make();
    let core = coreOf(region);
    // The first block has exactly 'bytes' of room (at least a 4096-byte block's),
    // and the next doubles from that, to 1 MiB
    var next = bytes;
    if next > maxBlockBytes { next = maxBlockBytes; }
    if next < blockBytes { next = blockBytes; }
    core.nextRoom = next;
    core.grow(bytes);
    Arena(region)
  }

  // Move value into the arena, and a reference to it there. The reference is
  // good for as long as the arena lives, and is checked as any borrow is, with
  // the holes the top of this file lists. The placing is done by the methods of
  // ArenaCore, which are not generic, so each instance of this one is only the
  // few lines that differ by T.
  //
  // This is 'inline' so that a value that is a construction, 'a.alloc(new
  // Node(1))' and so 'new a[Node](1)', is built where it stays: the call's
  // arguments are evaluated, the room is reserved, and the construction runs in
  // that room, so there is no copy of the value from somewhere else into the
  // arena. (The compiler does this for an inline function's parameter that the
  // function uses once, as the value of a mem.writeRaw statement of its own:
  // the construction waits for that statement. compiler/c/doc/phases/generation.md,
  // "A construction built where an inline function puts it".) The value is put on
  // the finalizer list after it is built, so the list's order is what it was.
  pub fn alloc[T](self: &mut, value: T) &mut T inline {
    let core = coreOf(region);
    let at = if mem.needsFinal[T]() {
      core.reserveFinal(mem.sizeof[T](), mem.alignof[T]())
    }
    else {
      core.place(mem.sizeof[T](), mem.alignof[T]())
    };
    mem.writeRaw(at as *T, value);
    if mem.needsFinal[T]() {
      core.linkFinal(at, (mem.typeRecord[T]() as &TypeRecord).finalize);
    }
    // A value holding a traced reference is put on the list a collector walks
    if mem.holdsTraced[T]() {
      core.noteTraced(at, mem.typeRecord[T]());
    }
    at as &mut T
  }

  // How a collector reaches what the arena holds, whatever the types: the values
  // whose type holds a traced reference, each as an Erased value, which is traced
  // by its type's own trace. An arena holding none gives none.
  pub fn gcTraceElements(self: &mut) ArenaGcTraceItems { coreOf(region).gcTraceElements() }

  // How many values have been given a finalizer header, and how many blocks
  // it holds: for tests and for tuning, not for correctness
  pub fn finalizers(self: &) usize { coreOf(region).nfinals }
  pub fn blockCount(self: &) usize { coreOf(region).nblocks }

  // Its death: the values and blocks of its state, then the state
  fn final(self: &uni) {
    let core = coreOf(region);
    core.teardown();
    libc.free(region as *u8);
  }
}

// A dynamic arena (the top of this file): an Arena whose values are reached by
// keys of its invariant lifetime ''=a', which only it unlocks. It holds the
// Arena that does the work, so it is allocated, finalized and freed exactly
// as a scratch is: its values die newest first when it does, then its blocks.
// Its first field is the Arena's region, the same one, so what 'new d[T](...)'
// makes in it draws from it.
pub struct DynArena['=a] is NoLoanMut {
  pub region: *MemRegion;
  ar: Arena;

  // An arena holding nothing, of a brand of its own: ''=a' is named by no
  // parameter, so each call mints a fresh one for its caller
  pub fn empty() DynArena['=a] {
    let a = Arena();
    DynArena(a.region, a)
  }

  // The same, its first block allocated now with room for 'bytes' of values
  pub fn withRoom(bytes: usize) DynArena['=a] {
    let a = Arena.withRoom(bytes);
    DynArena(a.region, a)
  }

  // Move value into the arena, and a key to it there: good for as long as
  // the arena lives, wherever it goes, and only through this arena
  //
  // TRUSTED (Bug 51): the inner arena is reached through a raw pointer, so the
  // checker does not see the store of 'value' into it. The promise: a value
  // holding a borrow never reaches here, because every route into 'alloc' meets
  // the key rule (ErrorKeyBorrow, lifetime_inv_key_holds_borrow) at the call.
  pub fn alloc[T](self: &mut, value: T) &'=a mut T inline {
    let inner = (&mut ar as *Arena) as &mut Arena;
    (inner.alloc(value) as *T) as &'=a mut T
  }

  // 'ar[key]': the value a key of this arena names, read
  pub fn `[]`[T](self: &, key: &'=a T) &T {
    (key as *T) as &T
  }

  // 'ar[key] = v', '&mut ar[key]', and a method taking 'self: &mut' through it:
  // the value a key of this arena names, to change
  pub fn `&[]`[T](self: &mut, key: &'=a T) &mut T {
    (key as *T) as &mut T
  }

  // How many values have been given a finalizer header, and how many blocks
  // it holds: for tests and for tuning
  pub fn finalizers(self: &) usize { ar.finalizers() }
  pub fn blockCount(self: &) usize { ar.blockCount() }
}

// Twice n, or the most a usize holds if that is more
fn doubled(n: usize) usize {
  if n > (0usize - 1usize) / 2usize {0usize - 1usize} else {n * 2usize}
}

// A new block with 'room' bytes after its header, the block before it 'prev'.
// A block the raw blocks layer cannot give, or whose size does not fit in a
// usize, is a panic.
fn newBlock(prev: *Block, room: usize) *Block {
  if room > (0usize - 1usize) - mem.sizeof[Block]() {
    panic("arena: a block that large cannot be counted in a usize");
  }
  let blk = RawBlocks.alloc(mem.sizeof[Block]() + room, 16usize) as *Block;
  if blk as usize == 0usize { panic("arena: out of memory for a block"); }
  mem.writeRaw(blk, Block(prev));
  blk
}

// n rounded up to a multiple of align, a power of two
fn roundUp(n: usize, align: usize) usize inline {
  (n + align - 1usize) & (0usize - align)
}

// The arena's region gives exactly what is asked (the region's size function)
fn regionSize(r: *MemRegion, bytes: usize, align: usize) usize { bytes }
